<BaseLayout title="About" description="About Ashish Nandan Singh: frontend platform engineer, framework tinkerer, Neovim person, and learning-in-public enthusiast.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>

    <p>
      Hey, I'm <strong>Ashish Nandan Singh</strong>. I am an engineer, reader, and
      writer with a lifelong inability to leave interesting machinery alone. At
      work, that usually means <mark>frontend platform</mark> systems: internal
      development frameworks, developer foundations, and the runtime architecture
      that lets product teams build on top of them without having to rediscover
      the same traps every quarter.
    </p>

    <p>
      I am also the kind of person who left VS Code for <mark>Neovim</mark> and
      then made it part of his personality, which is both a warning and a useful debugging
      detail. Most of my writing starts with one practical question: how does
      this thing actually work under the surface?
    </p>

    <h2>What I work on</h2>

    <p>
      Day to day, I help build and maintain the framework layer other developers
      use as their starting point for web, infrastructure, and product work. A
      lot of my recent time has gone into figuring out how microfrontends can
      work with <mark>Next.js</mark>: remote components, RSC payloads, shared modules,
      protected proxying, Shadow DOM isolation, and the messy seams between
      independently built applications.
    </p>

    <p>
      The work is less "regular app development" and more research, prototyping,
      debugging, and shaping platform contracts that other teams can trust. That
      is the kind of software I like most: the kind where the problem is not
      fully known until you start pulling it apart.
    </p>

    <p>
      Outside of work, I am building <mark>Fluffy</mark>, a tiny React framework
      for learning how routing, server rendering, and hydration fit together. It
      is not meant to replace production frameworks. It is my way of taking the
      machine apart carefully enough that the pieces become teachable.
    </p>

    <h2>Why I write</h2>

    <p>
      Writing is how I turn vague understanding into something sharper. If an
      idea took me a while to untangle, I would rather leave behind the map than
      keep the lesson trapped in my own head.
    </p>

    <h2>Get in touch</h2>

    <p>
      You can find me on
      <a href="https://github.com/sirdarthvader" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>
      and
      <a href="https://linkedin.com/in/ashish-nandan-singh" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
